### What is the core problem?

Multiple users edit the same document concurrently. Edits are operations like "insert 'x' at position 5". Positions are relative to the document state, so concurrent edits applied in different orders produce different results. You need convergence: all replicas end identical.

```java
package com.bank.collab.model;

public class TextOperation {
    enum Type { INSERT, DELETE }
    private Type type;
    private int position;
    private String character;
    private String userId;

    public TextOperation(Type type, int position, String character, String userId) {
        this.type = type;
        this.position = position;
        this.character = character;
        this.userId = userId;
    }

    public Type getType() { return type; }
    public int getPosition() { return position; }
    public void setPosition(int p) { this.position = p; }
}
```

### How does Operational Transformation work?

OT keeps operations position-based but transforms them against each other. If A inserted before B's target, B's position shifts.

```java
package com.bank.collab.ot;

import com.bank.collab.model.TextOperation;
import com.bank.collab.model.TextOperation.Type;

public class OperationalTransformer {

    public static TextOperation transform(TextOperation incoming, TextOperation applied) {
        TextOperation transformed = new TextOperation(
            incoming.getType(), incoming.getPosition(),
            incoming.getCharacter(), incoming.getUserId());

        if (applied.getType() == Type.INSERT) {
            if (incoming.getPosition() >= applied.getPosition()) {
                transformed.setPosition(incoming.getPosition() + 1);
            }
        } else if (applied.getType() == Type.DELETE) {
            if (incoming.getPosition() > applied.getPosition()) {
                transformed.setPosition(incoming.getPosition() - 1);
            }
        }
        return transformed;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Rajesh inserts 'A' at position 0
        TextOperation rajeshOp = new TextOperation(Type.INSERT, 0, "A", "Rajesh");
        // Priya deletes at position 5 (concurrent)
        TextOperation priyaOp = new TextOperation(Type.DELETE, 5, "", "Priya");

        // After applying Rajesh's insert, Priya's position must shift right by 1
        TextOperation transformedPriya = transform(priyaOp, rajeshOp);
        System.out.println("Priya's original position: " + priyaOp.getPosition());
        System.out.println("Priya's transformed position: " + transformedPriya.getPosition());
    }
}
```

```text
Priya's original position: 5
Priya's transformed position: 6
```

### How do CRDTs work?

Each character gets a globally unique, ordered ID. The document is the set of identified characters sorted by their IDs. Operations commute by construction.

```java
package com.bank.collab.crdt;

import java.util.TreeMap;
import java.util.UUID;

public class SimpleCrdtDocument {

    private final TreeMap<String, Character> chars = new TreeMap<>();

    public void insert(String positionId, char c) {
        chars.put(positionId, c);
    }

    public void delete(String positionId) {
        chars.remove(positionId); // tombstone in real impl
    }

    public String render() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        chars.values().forEach(sb::append);
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleCrdtDocument doc = new SimpleCrdtDocument();
        // Rajesh inserts 'H' with ID "a1"
        doc.insert("a1", 'H');
        // Priya inserts 'i' with ID "a2" (after a1)
        doc.insert("a2", 'i');
        // Order of inserts does not matter: IDs determine position
        System.out.println(doc.render()); // "Hi"
    }
}
```

```text
Hi
```

### What is the OT vs CRDT trade-off?

| Aspect | OT | CRDT |
|---|---|---|
| Data model | Plain text + positions | Characters with unique IDs |
| Central server | Required for ordering | Not required |
| Offline editing | Needs transform on reconnect | Merges cleanly (ops commute) |
| Metadata overhead | Low | Higher (ID per character + tombstones) |
| Complexity | Transform functions are hard to prove correct | Data structure design is complex |
| Used by | Classic Google Docs | Yjs, Automerge |

### How do you persist the op log and snapshots?

The ordered operation log is the authoritative history. Periodic snapshots accelerate recovery.

```java
package com.bank.collab.persistence;

import java.util.List;

public class OpLogStore {

    public void appendOp(String docId, TextOperation op) {
        // Append to ordered log
    }

    public String loadDocument(String docId) {
        // Load latest snapshot
        String snapshot = loadSnapshot(docId);
        // Replay ops since snapshot
        List<TextOperation> ops = loadOpsSinceSnapshot(docId);
        return applyOps(snapshot, ops);
    }

    private String loadSnapshot(String d) { return ""; }
    private List<TextOperation> loadOpsSinceSnapshot(String d) { return List.of(); }
    private String applyOps(String s, List<TextOperation> ops) { return s; }
}
```

### How does WebSocket sync work?

Edits are applied optimistically on the client for a snappy feel, sent over the socket, merged via OT or CRDT, and broadcast to all other clients.

```java
package com.bank.collab.sync;

public class CollabSyncFlow {
    // 1. Rajesh types a character -> applied locally immediately (optimistic)
    // 2. Operation sent to server via WebSocket
    // 3. Server applies OT/CRDT merge, assigns global order
    // 4. Merged result broadcast to Priya's WebSocket
    // 5. Priya's client applies the operation
    // Result: both see the same document
}
```

### How does offline editing work?

Buffer local operations while disconnected. On reconnect, replay them.

```java
package com.bank.collab.offline;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class OfflineBuffer {

    private final List<TextOperation> bufferedOps = new ArrayList<>();
    private boolean connected = true;

    public void onEdit(TextOperation op) {
        if (!connected) {
            bufferedOps.add(op);
        }
    }

    public List<TextOperation> onReconnect() {
        List<TextOperation> toReplay = new ArrayList<>(bufferedOps);
        bufferedOps.clear();
        connected = true;
        return toReplay;
        // OT: transform against missed ops
        // CRDT: simply merge (ops commute)
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you implement undo in a collaborative editor? How do you handle rich text (bold, italic, links)? What is intention preservation? How do you garbage-collect tombstones in a CRDT? How would you add commenting and suggesting modes?
